    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d214-a12">

                <pb facs="0519" n="423" sameAs="frus:frus1879/pg423"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Inclosure 7 in Mr. Hathorne’s No.
                    42.]</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">From John Kirk, esq., Her Majesty’s agent and consul
                        general, Zanzibar.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Zanzibar</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1879-01-02"><hi rendition="#italic">January</hi> 2,
                        1879</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to acknowledge the receipt
                    of your letter of the 31st December last, with inclosures, relative to the visit
                    and search, by order of Captain Earle, commanding Her Majesty’s ship London, of
                    the American bark Laconia.</p>
                <p>It is not necessary for me to discuss the legality or illegality of Captain
                    Earle’s action on this occasion. I must, however, be allowed to express regret
                    that if, under the circumstances, the search was illegal, it should have ever
                    taken place; and that, on the other hand, if legal, a vessel against which, on
                    the admissions contained in the statements which accompany your letter under
                    acknowledgment so strong a <hi rendition="#italic">primafacie</hi> proof of
                    slave-trading existed, should not have been taken to a court of
                    adjudication.</p>
                <p>I have the honor, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>
                        <list>
                            <item>JOHN KIRK,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Her Majesty’s Agent and
                                    Consul-General, Zanzibar.</hi></item>
                            <item><hi rendition="#small-caps">W. H. Hathorne</hi>, Esq.,<lb/><hi
                                    rendition="#italic">United States Consul.</hi></item>
                        </list>
                    </signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
